# Modern C++ Standards —

## 🎯 Core Philosophy

### Fundamental Principles

- **Zero-Cost Abstractions**: Use C++20 features that compile to optimal machine code without runtime overhead
- **Type Safety**: Enforce compile-time type safety using concepts, constexpr, and designated initializers
- **RAII**: All resources must be acquired in constructors and released in destructors automatically
- **Move Semantics**: Use perfect forwarding and structured bindings for efficient data handling
- **Const Correctness**: Default to const and constexpr wherever possible
  - Const references for function parameters to avoid unnecessary copies
  - Const methods to indicate read-only operations
  - Const local variables to prevent accidental modification
  - Const member variables for immutable class state
- **Business/ROS2 Separation**: Domain logic must never depend on ROS2 types or functions

### C++20 Features for Simplicity

- **Concepts**: Replace complex SFINAE with readable concept constraints
- **Ranges**: Use ranges views for functional, lazy data processing pipelines
- **Designated Initializers**: Use for clear, self-documenting object construction
- **constexpr**: Maximize compile-time computation for better performance
- **std::span**: Use for safe, non-owning array access
- **Three-way Comparison**: Use spaceship operator (<=>) for automatic comparison operators

## 🏗️ Clean Architecture Structure

### Mandatory Project Organization

- **Domain Layer**: Pure business logic in src/domain/ (entities, value_objects, interfaces)
- **Use Cases Layer**: Application business rules in src/use_cases/ (interfaces, implementations)
- **Adapters Layer**: Interface conversion in src/adapters/ (ros2, repositories, presenters)
- **Frameworks Layer**: External tools in src/frameworks/ (nodes, launch, config)
- **Entry Point**: main.cpp contains only initialization and framework setup
- **Public Headers**: All public interfaces in include/[package_name]/
- **CUDA Code**: Specialized GPU code in cuda/ (memory, kernels, host)
- **Tests**: Organized by architecture layer in test/

### Architecture Layer Rules for Business/ROS2 Separation

- **Domain**: Pure C++ business logic - NO rclcpp includes, NO ROS messages, NO ROS types
- **Use Cases**: Application workflows using only domain interfaces and standard C++ types
- **Adapters**: Convert between domain objects and ROS2 messages using explicit conversion functions
- **Frameworks**: All ROS2-specific code (nodes, publishers, subscribers, services)
- **Dependency Rule**: ROS2 types confined to frameworks layer only

### Business Logic Isolation Requirements

- **Domain Classes**: Use only standard C++ types (std::string, std::vector, std::chrono, etc.)
- **Value Objects**: Immutable business data with C++20 designated initializers
- **Business Rules**: Expressed in pure C++ without any ROS2 dependencies
- **Error Types**: Domain-specific error enums, not ROS2 status codes
- **Data Transfer**: Use standard C++ containers between layers

### Namespace Organization

- **domain::amr**: Core AMR business logic (navigation, intervention, supervision)
- **domain::perception**: Fire detection and thermal analysis logic
- **domain::actuation**: Turret, cannon, and pump control logic
- **use_cases::mission**: Mission execution workflows
- **adapters::ros2**: ROS2 message conversion and communication
- **frameworks::nodes**: ROS2 node implementations

## 🚀 C++20 Features for Ease of Use

### Simple and Readable Concepts

- **Domain Concepts**: Define intuitive concepts (ThermalData, NavigationPoint, FireEvent)
- **Readable Constraints**: Use clear requires clauses that express business intent
- **Type Safety**: Prevent template misuse with meaningful error messages
- **Self-Documenting**: Concepts that read like business requirements

### Ranges for Data Processing

- **Thermal Data Processing**: Use ranges views for image/thermal data pipelines
- **Mission Planning**: Process waypoints and trajectories with functional style
- **Sensor Fusion**: Combine multiple data streams using ranges algorithms
- **Error-Safe Pipelines**: Use ranges with std::optional for safe data processing
- **Memory Efficient**: Lazy evaluation prevents unnecessary data copies

### Modern Value Types with Designated Initializers

- **Clear Construction**: Use designated initializers for configuration objects
- **Self-Documenting Code**: Parameter names visible at call site
- **Default Values**: Combine with default member initializers
- **Aggregate Types**: Prefer aggregates over complex constructors for data classes
- **Validation**: Use constexpr functions for compile-time validation

### constexpr for Compile-Time Computation

- **Configuration**: Compute robot parameters at compile time
- **Lookup Tables**: Generate thermal thresholds and conversion tables
- **Mathematical Constants**: All robot-specific constants computed at compile time
- **Type Traits**: Use constexpr for domain-specific type checking
- **Performance**: Eliminate runtime computation for known values

## 🔧 Memory Management & Performance

### Simple and Safe Memory Management

- **std::unique_ptr**: Default choice for single ownership, automatic cleanup
- **std::shared_ptr**: Only when multiple owners needed (sensor calibration data, configurations)
- **std::span**: For safe array access without ownership (thermal image data, waypoint arrays)
- **Stack Allocation**: Prefer stack objects for temporary data processing
- **RAII Everywhere**: Hardware resources (cameras, CAN controllers) wrapped in RAII classes

### Efficient Data Handling with C++20

- **Structured Bindings**: Decompose sensor data and coordinates clearly
- **Perfect Forwarding**: Use for efficient sensor data pipelines
- **Move Semantics**: Essential for large thermal images and point clouds
- **std::optional**: For optional sensor readings and navigation results
- **Copy Elision**: Rely on guaranteed copy elision for return values

### Simple Thread Safety

- **std::atomic**: For simple flags and counters (robot state, mission status)
- **std::mutex**: For protecting shared resources (sensor calibration, mission data)
- **std::shared_mutex**: For read-heavy data (configuration, lookup tables)
- **Thread-Safe Initialization**: Use std::call_once for expensive initialization
- **Lock-Free Design**: Prefer message passing over shared mutable state

## 🔒 Simple and Safe Error Handling

### Business-Friendly Error Types

- **Domain Errors**: NavigationError, ThermalError, InterventionError, MissionError
- **std::expected**: Use for operations that can fail (sensor reading, navigation planning)
- **Error Chaining**: Use and_then() for sequential operations that can fail
- **Early Returns**: Use std::expected to avoid deep nesting
- **Clear Error Messages**: Each error carries human-readable description

### Defensive Programming

- **Input Validation**: Check all parameters at function entry points
- **Preconditions**: Use assert() or custom macros for debug-time checks
- **Postconditions**: Validate function results in debug builds
- **Invariants**: Check object state consistency in debug mode
- **Fail Fast**: Detect problems early rather than propagating invalid state

### Exception Safety for Simplicity

- **RAII Only**: Use RAII for all resources, avoid explicit exception handling
- **Strong Guarantee**: Operations either succeed completely or leave unchanged state
- **No-Throw Destructors**: All cleanup code marked noexcept
- **Resource Safety**: Hardware interfaces wrapped in RAII classes
- **Simple Recovery**: Log errors and return to safe state

## 🧪 Testing & Validation

### Modern Testing Requirements

- **Framework**: Use GTest for all unit and integration tests
- **Layer Testing**: Test each Clean Architecture layer independently
- **Mock Objects**: Use Google Mock for dependency injection testing
- **Property Testing**: Include property-based tests for algorithmic code
- **Performance Testing**: Use benchmarking for performance-critical code

### Test Organization

- **Test Structure**: Mirror source directory structure in test/
- **Naming**: Use descriptive test names with domain/feature tags
- **Sections**: Use SECTION for multiple test scenarios
- **Assertions**: Use REQUIRE for critical checks, CHECK for additional validation
- **Matchers**: Use Catch2 matchers for floating-point and complex comparisons

## 🎨 Code Style & Conventions

### Naming Standards

- **Variables/Functions**: snake_case (current_position, calculate_distance)
- **Classes/Types**: PascalCase (RobotController, NavigationState)
- **Concepts**: PascalCase (Serializable, Movable)
- **Constants**: kScreamingSnakeCase (kMaxSpeed, kDefaultTimeout)
- **Private Members**: trailing underscore (internal*state*, resource\_)
- **Namespaces**: snake_case matching directory structure

### File Organization Requirements

- **Header Guards**: Use #pragma once in all header files
- **Include Order**: Standard library, third-party, local headers
- **Documentation**: Doxygen comments for all public interfaces
- **Forward Declarations**: Use forward declarations to minimize dependencies
- **Explicit Constructors**: Mark single-parameter constructors explicit

### Function Design

- **Return Types**: Use trailing return type syntax for complex types
- **Parameters**:
  - **const reference** (`const T&`) for read-only parameters of non-trivial types (classes, structs, containers)
  - **Value** (`T`) for cheap-to-copy types (primitives, small aggregates, trivially copyable types)
  - **Move semantics** (`T&&` or rvalue reference) for taking ownership of large objects
  - **std::span** for non-owning array/container views
- **Const Correctness**:
  - Mark function parameters `const` when they are not modified
  - Use `const` methods for read-only operations on objects
  - Mark local variables `const` when they are not reassigned
  - Prefer `const` member variables when values don't change after construction
- **Attributes**: Use [[nodiscard]] for all functions returning values
- **Noexcept**: Mark all non-throwing functions noexcept
- **Overloads**: Provide const and non-const overloads where appropriate

## 🚄 CUDA Integration

### CUDA Memory Management

- **RAII Wrappers**: Use RAII for all CUDA memory allocations
- **Custom Deleters**: Implement CudaDeleter for automatic cleanup
- **Error Handling**: Use std::expected for CUDA operation results
- **Memory Pools**: Implement memory pools for frequent allocations
- **Type Safety**: Use typed pointers with custom deleters

### CUDA Code Organization

- **Kernel Separation**: One file per kernel type (processing, filtering, segmentation)
- **Host Wrappers**: Provide C++ wrapper functions for all CUDA kernels
- **Error Checking**: Centralize CUDA error checking in dedicated handlers
- **Performance Monitoring**: Include automatic profiling for all kernel launches
- **Device Functions**: Separate device functions in dedicated files

## 🔄 ROS 2 Integration with Clean Separation

### Strict Layer Isolation

- **Domain Independence**: Business logic never includes rclcpp headers
- **Adapter Pattern**: Explicit conversion functions between ROS types and domain types
- **Message Translation**: Convert geometry_msgs::Pose to domain::Position in adapters
- **Error Translation**: Convert domain errors to ROS service responses
- **Type Safety**: Use strong types to prevent ROS/domain type mixing

### Simple ROS 2 Node Design

- **Constructor Injection**: Inject domain use cases into node constructors
- **Single Responsibility**: Each node handles one specific ROS interface
- **Message Validation**: Validate incoming ROS messages before domain conversion
- **Error Logging**: Use RCLCPP\_\* macros only in frameworks layer
- **Lifecycle Management**: Use RAII for ROS subscriptions and publishers

### Efficient Message Handling

- **Zero-Copy When Possible**: Use std::move for large data transfers
- **Structured Bindings**: Decompose ROS messages clearly
- **std::span**: Access message arrays without copying
- **Batch Processing**: Process multiple messages efficiently
- **Memory Pools**: Reuse message objects for high-frequency topics

## 📏 Performance with Simplicity

### C++20 Performance Features

- **constexpr**: Compute robot constants at compile time
- **consteval**: Force compile-time evaluation for critical calculations
- **Ranges Views**: Zero-cost lazy evaluation for data processing
- **std::span**: Avoid copying for array parameters
- **Move Semantics**: Essential for thermal images and sensor data

### Simple Performance Patterns

- **Stack First**: Use stack allocation for temporary objects
- **Pool Reuse**: Reuse objects for high-frequency operations (message processing)
- **Batch Processing**: Process sensor data in batches, not individual readings
- **Cache-Friendly**: Keep related data together (sensor readings with timestamps)
- **Lazy Loading**: Load configuration and calibration data only when needed

### Easy Profiling and Optimization

- **Built-in Timing**: Use std::chrono for simple performance measurement
- **Debug Assertions**: Check performance assumptions in debug builds
- **Memory Tracking**: Monitor allocation patterns for sensor data processing
- **Hot Path Identification**: Profile typical mission execution scenarios
- **Realistic Testing**: Use actual thermal data and mission scenarios for benchmarks

